# Nothing leaves compiled code, and every change is one that Link reads: behind the first object a name with a text is laid on the prototype object and the method is stored over; another function gets a literal as its `prototype`.
# A name under a symbol, and `call` where one of the two is an async function, are laid on one of two closures: Link follows neither name, so neither function is taken to stay as made.
# A prototype object that nobody has touched yet, a class object, a closure and a constructor function are frozen: every member is defined again as it is, but for being read-only.
# `Keyed` stores nothing by name and nobody looks a name up through its objects, so a definition under a key that is worked out lets nothing leave. It lays another function under `size` behind the first object: where such a definition stands no proof speaks of a name, and no name is held to its function.
# RUN: run-in-shell
# CHECK: weigh 1 undefined
# CHECK-NEXT: again 1 laid
# CHECK-NEXT: 1
# CHECK-NEXT: swapped
# CHECK-NEXT: marked 1 2 own call
# CHECK-NEXT: weigh make frozen 1 2 true true true
# CHECK-NEXT: false true
# CHECK-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(CALLED): run-in-shell mechanisms=all-Inlining
# CALLED: weigh 1 undefined
# CALLED-NEXT: again 1 laid
# CALLED-NEXT: 1
# CALLED-NEXT: swapped
# CALLED-NEXT: marked 1 2 own call
# CALLED-NEXT: weigh make frozen 1 2 true true true
# CALLED-NEXT: false true
# CALLED-NEXT: exited with 0
